:: day1_npc_aldric_door [nobr]
<<npcPortrait "aldric" "Aldric">>
<h3>Aldric's Voice Through the Door</h3>

<p>I hear him before I recognise him. Boots on stone — heavy, even, the cadence of a man who has never been in a hurry. The specific footstep pattern of Sir Aldric Vane, which I have been hearing since I was fourteen and which my brain identifies with the speed and certainty of a body recognising its own heartbeat.</p>

<p>He stops outside my door. I know it's him the way I know the ceiling crack and the morning cold — through accumulated data, the slow geological formation of familiarity.</p>

<p>He doesn't knock.</p>

<p>He stands outside the door for what must be three minutes. I can hear him breathing — the steady, measured breathing of a man who controls his respiration the way he controls his sword arm: deliberately, with discipline, as a matter of professional practice.</p>

<p>Then his voice. Low. Through the oak.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ALDRIC</span>"Your Highness."</div>

<p>Two words. The same two words he's said to me a thousand times — in the training yard, in the corridor, at the palace gate. The same intonation, the same respectful formality. He hasn't changed the address. He hasn't adjusted the register. He's calling me <em>Your Highness</em> the way he always has, because the rank hasn't changed even if the person holding it has, and Aldric's loyalty is to the title and the person and neither requires a specific set of chromosomes.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ALDRIC</span>"I'm told you're resting." His voice through the door. I'm standing on the other side. My hand is on the wood. I can feel the vibration of his voice through the oak. "I won't disturb you. I only wanted you to know — the practice sword is on the yard rack. It'll be there when you're ready. No rush."</div>

<p>A pause. The breathing.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ALDRIC</span>"You're still my prince." Another pause. He corrects himself — not because someone told him to, but because he's working it out in real time, standing in a corridor talking to a door. "My princess. My... you're still mine to protect. Whatever you need. I'm down the corridor."</div>

<p>His boots. Walking away. Heavy, even, certain. The sound fades.</p>

<p>I stand with my hand on the door. The wood is warm where I've been pressing. My hand — this new hand, slender and small — flat against the oak, and on the other side, three minutes ago, Aldric's hand might have been doing the same thing.</p>

<p>He called me his. He stumbled on the word — prince to princess, the linguistic adjustment lagging behind the emotional one — and the stumble was the most genuine thing I've heard today because it meant he was trying, in real time, to meet me where I am, and where I am is a place he's never been and can't fully understand and he's coming anyway.</p>

<<set $d1f_npc_aldric_door to true>>
<<set $affAldric += 3>>
<<set $statRES += 2>>
<<set $stress -= 5>>
<<completeObjective "aldric_door">>

